feat(deploy): give the backup its own database role (#237)

The backup is moving off the host scheduler and into the deployment, and the
credential it holds today is the reason that matters: it reaches the database
through the container runtime, so the thing taking backups holds a socket that
is root on the machine. A service that is up as long as the stack is cannot
hold that.

`innsegl_backup` is the reader's grants plus CREATEDB, and CREATEDB only
because the backup proves a dump restores by restoring it — into a throwaway
database of its own, never the live one. internal/api/readonly.sql is applied
to it unchanged, so the two roles cannot drift about what reading the ledger
means, and the CREATEDB comes after it, because that file's last line revokes
it.

Undoing a line of somebody else's grants file is only safe because nothing is
trusted: verify-backup-role.sh asks the SERVER, and provisioning gates on the
answer. The positive case is asserted first — a scope too narrow to take a
backup fails at 3am in a service nobody is watching, which is worse than the
exposure it closes.

Measured against the deployment. The whole job is allowed; INSERT, UPDATE,
DELETE, TRUNCATE and CREATE TABLE on the ledger are each refused 42501 — by
privilege, which is the only refusal that counts. The database owner is refused
those too, by the triggers, and that is the confusion the SQLSTATE check exists
to catch.

One measurement cost a wrong diagnosis on the way and is written down in the
script: the role carries default_transaction_read_only=on, so CREATE DATABASE
is refused 25006 even with rolcreatedb=t. That says nothing about the
privilege, and reading it as one sends you looking for a GRANT that is already
there.

